# {{project_name}}

{{project_description}}

This native SwiftUI application has a normal window, a menu bar item, a toolbar,
keyboard commands and a settings window. The starter workspace counter persists
its state through an injected file store. First launch creates a new workspace;
corrupt saved data is reported rather than replaced with a default.

## Install, build and launch

Use an Apple Silicon Mac running macOS 26.6 or newer with **Xcode 27, build
27A266a**, selected by `xcode-select`. Applications target macOS 26.0. The Swift
compiler is Xcode's bundled Swift 6.4 in Swift 6 language mode. Full Xcode is a
prerequisite because Apple's download, licensing and first-launch setup cannot
be completed anonymously by this template.

Copier runs `just init` automatically. It installs every selected command-line
tool into `.tools/`, verifies download checksums, creates a private Python runtime
for Semgrep/codespell, resolves the exact Swift package dependencies, builds the
SwiftSyntax checker and installs the pre-commit hook. Initial setup also measures
launch performance on this runner and saves the initial baseline. It requires a
working graphical session. Existing baselines are never refreshed by setup or CI.
No tools are optional, and
missing tools fail checks instead of falling back to global installations.

```sh
just init
just ci
just run
```

The generated Xcode project is derived from `project.yml`. Edit that specification
and Swift files directly; no Xcode editor is required. `just build` produces
`build/DerivedData/Build/Products/Debug/{{project_name}}.app`. `just run` validates
and launches that bundle with `open`. `just debug` opens the executable in LLDB.

## Quality commands

| Command | What it checks |
| --- | --- |
| `just check` | Exact host and installed tool versions |
| `just code-format` | Explicit formatting operation; never invoked by CI |
| `just code-style` | Non-mutating Swift format checks and strict SwiftLint |
| `just code-analyze` | Clean compiler log and type-aware unused code/import checks |
| `just code-architecture` | SwiftSyntax policies and declared module dependencies |
| `just code-semgrep` | Forbidden shell execution and shell-script policies |
| `just code-security` | Application security patterns |
| `just code-secrets` | Gitleaks scans of project-owned source/configuration |
| `just code-shell` | ShellCheck and shfmt for automation |
| `just code-spell` | Spelling in source, tests, scripts and guidance |
| `just code-dependencies` | Locked dependency resolution and OSV advisories |
| `just code-docs` | DocC documentation diagnostics |
| `just code-package` | Bundle metadata, architecture, signature and transport policy |
| `just test` | Swift Testing unit/integration/property tests and view inspection |
| `just test-ui` | Actual application interaction through XCUIAutomation |
| `just test-accessibility` | Native accessibility audits |
| `just test-snapshots` | Reviewed snapshot comparisons |
| `just test-coverage` | At least 80% line coverage in each named first-party framework |
| `just test-sanitize` | Separate AddressSanitizer and ThreadSanitizer runs |
| `just test-performance` | Ten launch measurements, compared with the runner baseline (20% tolerance) |
| `just performance-baseline` | Explicitly recalibrate after an intentional runner or performance change |
| `just coverage-export PROFILE BINARY` | Inspect a profile with llvm-profdata and export it with llvm-cov |
| `just ci` | All mandatory quality and test commands, fail-fast |
| `just ci-quiet` | The same suite with a retained log |

OSV scanning requires network access to current vulnerability advisories. Scanner
and network failures fail CI. Coverage is line coverage, not branch coverage.
Build failures, empty test selections, skipped tests and expected test failures
are errors. Test results and logs are retained under `build/results/`.

UI tests need a logged-in macOS graphical session and permission for test
automation. A disconnected or locked runner cannot stand in for a working UI
test environment. Snapshot references are reviewable source artifacts; CI never
accepts new reference images automatically. Pin macOS, Xcode, appearance, locale
and view size when capturing or comparing images.

## Architecture

`App` assembles `AppUI`, `AppApplication` and `AppInfrastructure`. UI calls
application services; application services use `AppDomain` and persistence
interfaces. Infrastructure implements those interfaces. Domain and application
code cannot directly use filesystem, network, process-environment or preferences
APIs. External testing libraries are test dependencies, never app dependencies.
The custom checker enforces the declared module graph and specified syntax/API
policies. It is not a whole-program, type-resolved proof of every indirect effect.

## Profiling and distribution

`just profile` records a ten-second Time Profiler trace. `just memory PID` runs
`vmmap` and `leaks` for an explicitly selected running process. Runtime diagnostics
are separate from performance measurements. Establish performance baselines on
the intended runner; results from unrelated hardware are not interchangeable.

The local build uses ad-hoc signing and does not need distribution credentials.
`just release` requires `SIGNING_IDENTITY` and `NOTARY_PROFILE`, runs CI, archives
with hardened runtime, submits through `notarytool`, and staples the ticket.
Store notarization credentials in the keychain; never put them in configuration
files or source control.

Mutation testing is not configured pending the upstream-tool evaluation and the
template owner's decision. No mutation score is claimed by this template.

## Tool updates

`config/tools.lock.tsv` records native versions and SHA-256 downloads.
`config/python-tools.lock` pins and hashes Semgrep/codespell and their dependencies.
`project.yml` pins test libraries; `Tools/Guardrails/Package.swift` pins SwiftSyntax.
Update pins deliberately, regenerate dependency resolutions, and run all template
baseline and violation tests before accepting an update. Installing a newer Xcode
alone does not silently change the project's supported compiler.
